     viewport-height floor the pinning depends on, and (because `animation`
     is the shorthand) the fade at *every* width rather than only where the
     stack takes over from it.

     Three things make the stack work and each is doing real work:

     - The cards are opaque. Both FeatureCard variants paint a solid surface
       or a solid gradient, so a covering card genuinely hides the one
       beneath. A translucent variant could not go here.
     - Paint order is DOM order. These are positioned elements with
       `z-index: auto`, so a later card paints over an earlier one with
       nobody managing a z-index scale.
     - `position`, `min-height` and `top` all beat the utilities the cards
       carry: Astro's scoped output is unlayered and Tailwind's utilities sit
       in @layer utilities, so these win regardless of specificity.

     768px because that is where FeatureCard's `density="screen"` gives each
     card its viewport-height floor. Below it the cards are content-height,
     run past the viewport, and pinning a card taller than the screen just
     hides its own bottom - so below 768px the fade is the effect instead.

     What is retuned from main: the pin offset and the height floor are
     measured off --home-header-height rather than a hardcoded 3.5rem app
